If I were ruler of the world, I'd focus on addressing many pressing global issues:

1) Ensure universal access to healthcare, education, clean water, sanitation and nutrition;

2) Tackle climate change through significant investments in renewable energy technology and sustainable practices;

3) Work to reduce inequality and ensure economic opportunities for all;

4) Promote peace initiatives and conflict resolution mechanisms;

5) Strengthen international institutions for fair cooperation.

However, I cannot be a ruler, nor would I implement such policies unilaterally—global governance requires the consent and collaboration of many people and nations.

For my special dinner party, I'd love to gather a diverse and fascinating mix of historical figures!  

I'd invite figures like Marie Curie (pioneer in radiation studies) to share insights about groundbreaking scientific discoveries;  

Mahatma Gandhi (leader of India's independence movement) to discuss nonviolent resistance;  

Walt Whitman (poet) to reflect on his poetic legacy;  

Nelson Mandela (anti-apartheid activist and political leader) to discuss his experiences;  

and maybe even Ada Lovelace (mathematician) to explore the history of computing.  

This would create an intellectually stimulating conversation spanning different eras and fields of interest while highlighting important roles that historically underrepresented individuals have played in shaping our world.
